  <content>
  <section><title>Writing Quadratic Equations from Graphs</title>
<para>Write an equation for the graph below:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 8 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND 4."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c055716b4854e25b101b61523b81193fa53c6497"/></media></figure></para>
<para>First, locate the \(x\)-intercepts at \((-2,0)\) and \((4,0)\).</para>
<para>The factors that result in the given zeros are \((x + 2)\) and \((x - 4)\).</para>
<para>Use graphing technology to check your answer.</para>
<title>Try It: Writing Quadratic Equations from Graphs</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write an equation for the graph below:</para>
    <para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UPWARD WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 12 AND \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 6 AND 2."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/21d2c1a12d77163c8bb135021166ab7f3494cee3"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to write an equation for the function graphed:</para>
  <para>First, identify the \(x\)-intercepts: \(x = -6\), \(x = 2.\)</para>
  <para>The function in factored form is \(f(x)=(x + 6)(x - 2)\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
